// ONE shared Renovate policy for every consumer. A consumer's renovate.json is a single line:
//   { "extends": ["github>mjdevaccount/ci-kit//renovate/default.json5"] }
// A consumer may also extend a private preset that is itself one line extending this one.
// Either way a rule changes here, once, for all.
//
// WHY A PUBLIC REPO (2026-11-03). The policy used to live in a private repo. Renovate gives a
// public repo a token scoped to that repo alone, so it cannot read a preset in a private one
// (docs.renovatebot.com/getting-started/private-packages/): a public consumer opened "Cannot find
// preset's package" and stopped opening PRs. ci-kit is public, so every repo can read it. ONE
// copy: a private preset is a pointer here, never a second policy.
//
// WHY RENOVATE (2026-10-10). Dependency refreshes were a session's manual work: a lock refresh,
// a CVE bump, "held for operator call" lists that nobody revisited. Fleet collectors KNEW
// the hosts were behind and nothing turned that into a change. Renovate turns "a newer version
// exists" into a PR, and CI -- the merge gate everywhere -- decides whether it lands.
//
// THE MERGE GATE IS STILL CI -- EVERY CHECK, NOT ONLY THE REQUIRED ONES. GitHub's own auto-merge
// (`platformAutomerge`) waits only on the checks branch protection REQUIRES, and the checks that
// prove a dependency bump (a PR-time image build, a requirements resolve) are not required: the Claude app that runs sessions has no Administration permission,
// so branch protection stays a hand click. With platformAutomerge OFF Renovate merges itself, and
// only once every check run on the PR is green (`ignoreTests` is false; a skipped step is a real
// green). Nothing here merges past a red check, required or not. Cost: the merge lands on
// Renovate's next pass after the last check reports, not the second it does.
{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",
  extends: [
    "config:recommended",
    ":semanticCommits",
    ":dependencyDashboard",      // one issue per repo: everything pending, deferred, blocked
    ":rebaseStalePrs",
  ],
  timezone: "America/New_York",  // market logic is ET; so is the window below
  // Weekend only: a merge in some consumers DEPLOYS (an hourly deploy reconciler), and
  // the weekend is the quiet window. BOTH weekend days (2026-10-23): with a
  // Saturday-only window plus the 3-day age wait, a release after Wednesday noon slipped a week.
  // ALL OF SATURDAY, SUNDAY UNTIL 18:00 ET (2026-10-24). The window is a filter
  // on runs Mend starts, not a run of its own: Mend runs a "hot" repo (one that has merged a
  // Renovate PR) hourly but a "cold" one -- never merged one -- about ONCE A DAY. A consumer
  // onboarded 10-10 had no in-window run on Saturday 10-17, and so opened ZERO PRs in two
  // weeks; zero merges kept it cold, and cold kept it missing the window. Two 13-hour mornings
  // are not guaranteed to contain a once-a-day run; a 24-hour Saturday is. Sunday stops at 18:00
  // so the last auto-merge deploys (hourly) hours before Monday morning.
  schedule: ["* * * * 6", "* 0-17 * * 0"],
  // No day-0 releases: a broken or hijacked publish is usually yanked within hours.
  minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
  // THE timestamp-optional WARN IS DEMOTED TO INFO (2026-11-26). Renovate logs "Some release(s)
  // / upgrade(s) did not have a releaseTimestamp, but as we're running with
  // minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour=timestamp-optional, proceeding" ONCE per run whenever a rule here
  // set timestamp-optional and a candidate had no timestamp
  // (lookup/filter-checks.ts, update/branch/status-checks.ts) -- i.e. every run of every repo
  // with a non-Docker-Hub image (the registry rule in packageRules). It names no package and
  // reports only what this file chose, so it sat in two consumers' Repository Problems
  // permanently, beside the WARNs that DO mean something (a skipped pip-compile lock, hidden
  // unicode). Remapping is Renovate's documented remedy (minimum-release-age.md,
  // "timestamp-optional warning"); the line stays in Mend's log at info. Anchored to those two
  // messages only: the timestamp-REQUIRED hold (a debug line) and every other WARN are
  // untouched (tests/test_renovate_release_timestamp.py).
  logLevelRemap: [
    {
      matchMessage: "/^Some (release|upgrade)\\(s\\) did not have a releaseTimestamp, but as we're running with minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour=timestamp-optional, proceeding/",
      newLogLevel: "info",
    },
  ],
  internalChecksFilter: "strict",
  prConcurrentLimit: 15,
  prHourlyLimit: 0,
  labels: ["deps"],
  platformAutomerge: false,   // see the header: Renovate waits on EVERY check, then merges
  // `autoland-off` (the consumers' opt-out: a label or a title tag, honoured by the PR hooks, the
  // merge train and pr-autoupdate) is ALSO Renovate's stop label. Renovate 44 has no "skip automerge"
  // label, but a PR carrying stopUpdatingLabel returns before the automerge check
  // (workers/repository/update/branch/index.js), so it neither rebases nor merges it. A consumer's #2995
  // (2026-11-12 03:21:47Z) was merged by Renovate two minutes after a session disarmed it and added
  // `autoland-off`. Ticking the PR's rebase box overrides the label for that one pass.
  stopUpdatingLabel: "autoland-off",
  // A relock commit is still Renovate's branch. Renovate will not auto-merge (or rebase) a branch
  // carrying a commit it did not author, and the relock workflows (ci-kit relock.yml and its
  // predecessors) commit as github-actions[bot] -- a consumer's #221 sat CLEAN, every check green,
  // never merged (#228 added this there; with it #350/#352 merged themselves, 2026-11-01). Here,
  // once, so a newly wired repo cannot forget it. Only that bot: a human's commit on a Renovate
  // branch still stops the auto-merge, which is the point of the rule.
  gitIgnoredAuthors: ["41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com"],

  // Security fixes skip both the window and the age wait.
  vulnerabilityAlerts: {
    enabled: true,
    schedule: ["at any time"],
    minimumReleaseAge: null,
    automerge: true,
    labels: ["deps", "security"],
  },
  osvVulnerabilityAlerts: true,

  // The transitive refresh a session used to do by hand (`uv lock --upgrade`, npm audit fix).
  // NO AGE WAIT HERE (2026-10-31): a lock refresh delegates to the package manager, so the
  // packages it moves carry no release timestamp, and with the default
  // minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour (timestamp-required) the `renovate/stability-days` status stays
  // PENDING FOR AS LONG AS minimumReleaseAge IS SET -- Renovate's own words
  // (docs/usage/key-concepts/minimum-release-age.md, "lockFileMaintenance updates"; its
  // security:minimumReleaseAge* presets null it for lock file maintenance for that reason).
  // Measured: six lock-file PRs (in six consumer repos) sat 7 days with every CI check green and ONLY
  // stability-days pending; every rebase after a main merge regenerated the lock and the wait
  // began again. A wait that cannot end is a queue, not a gate. The age guard for a lock
  // refresh belongs to the package manager (npm --before / uv exclude-newer; renovate#41652),
  // not to a status that reads a timestamp no lock entry has. CI still gates the merge.
  //
  // ONCE A WEEK, ONE EARLY WINDOW (v1.35.0, 2026-11-14): Saturday 00:00-05:59 ET. Across the
  // whole weekend window every run re-generated the lock whenever upstream moved and pushed it
  // again (each push a full CI run), and a lock PR that landed opened the next one hours later.
  // Now at most one lock PR per repo per week is CREATED, in the window. Outside it the two kinds
  // of repo differ, on purpose:
  //   - a serial-queue repo (rule at the end) adds `updateNotScheduled: false`: the open lock PR
  //     is not re-pushed outside the window, so it cannot land mid merge-train compose; the queue
  //     lands it as-is. Renovate does not merge there anyway (`pr-comment`).
  //   - every other repo keeps the default (true): Renovate itself is the merger, and its
  //     automerge runs inside the same branch pass that `updateNotScheduled: false` would skip
  //     (lib/workers/repository/update/branch/index.ts returns `update-not-scheduled` before
  //     tryBranchAutomerge / checkAutoMerge), so `false` there would strand a green lock PR
  //     until next Saturday.
  // A conflict still gets one recreate (a rebase request bypasses the skip). Security fixes do
  // NOT ride this PR: vulnerabilityAlerts above opens its own PR at any time with no age wait.
  lockFileMaintenance: {
    enabled: true,
    schedule: ["* 0-5 * * 6"],
    automerge: true,
    minimumReleaseAge: null,
  },

  // ---- compiled, hash-locked Python requirements: `requirements.lock` (2026-11-08) ----------
  // A `uv pip compile --generate-hashes` output is a LOCK, not a hand file. pip_requirements read
  // a consumer's compiled requirements.txt as one, bumped `pydantic-core==` in place
  // KEEPING the old hashes, and appended a duplicate `pydantic_core==` block: the image build
  // (`pip install --require-hashes`) went red with THESE PACKAGES DO NOT MATCH THE HASHES
  // (a consumer, 2026-11-08). The pip-compile manager re-runs the command in the file's own header, so
  // every pin and hash moves together and transitive refreshes come via lockFileMaintenance.
  // THE CONVENTION IS THE NAME `requirements.lock`, because a manager cannot match on a header:
  //   - pip_requirements' default pattern ([\w-]*requirements([-._]\w+)?\.(txt|pip)) never matches
  //     `.lock`, so it is off these files by construction -- no exclusion rule to forget;
  //   - pip-compile on a file with NO header logs `WARN pip-compile error` per file, so it cannot
  //     simply take every requirements.txt (consumers keep hand-pinned ones too).
  // THE HEADER MUST BE ONE RENOVATE PARSES (lib/modules/manager/pip-compile/common.ts): long
  // options with `=` (`--output-file=requirements.lock`, `--python-version=3.14`), no `-o`, no
  // `--python-platform` (rejected "not supported (yet)"; use `--universal`). An unparseable header
  // is a WARN, not an error: the file is skipped and the lock goes stale with every check green.
  // A Renovate liveness check outside the kit should report that WARN as an error for that reason.
  "pip-compile": {
    managerFilePatterns: ["/(^|/)requirements\\.lock$/"],
  },

  // ---- first-party kit pins Renovate's own managers cannot see (2026-11-01) ----------------
  // ci-kit's ci-pin-drift (pin-drift.yml, a daily cron in each repo) DETECTS a pin older than its
  // kit's newest vX.Y.Z tag; Renovate is the ACTOR that should bump it. Until this block no
  // manager parsed the shapes those pins take outside `uses:`, so every kit release turned
  // pin-drift red and nothing opened a PR: four consumers (ci-kit v1.7.1 vs v1.10.2, two kits
  // v0.4.0 vs v0.5.0 / v0.6.0) sat red 2-8 days and were bumped by hand.
  // The shapes are the ones ci_kit/pin_drift.py `pin_re` reads -- change one, change the other.
  //   - `git+https://github.com/mjdevaccount/<kit>(.git)?@vX.Y.Z`: npm scripts (uvx --from), `run:`
  //     pip lines, PEP 723 headers, PowerShell. The `https://github.com/` prefix is REQUIRED so a
  //     `uses: mjdevaccount/<kit>@vX.Y.Z` stays with the github-actions manager, not two managers.
  //     Renovate's own pep621/pep723 managers do not cover these: their shared PEP 508 parser marks
  //     a direct-URL dependency `skipReason: invalid-value` (renovate 42.99.0 dry-run, 2026-11-01),
  //     so the regex below is the only manager that reads them -- no double PR.
  //   - `.../mjdevaccount/<kit>/releases/download/vX.Y.Z/<stem>-X.Y.Z-...` (a wheel no index
  //     publishes). The version is there TWICE; Renovate replaces currentValue everywhere inside
  //     the match (autoReplaceGlobalMatch, default true), so both move together. github-releases,
  //     not -tags: a tag whose release asset is not built yet would be a 404 URL.
  //   - a `.pin` file written `<kit>@vX.Y.Z` (e.g. `observer/my-kit.pin`). A .pin that
  //     holds a BARE tag with the kit only in its file name (e.g. `system/ci-kit.pin`) is NOT read here --
  //     nor by ci-pin-drift; write it `<kit>@vX.Y.Z` to bring it under both.
  // Every version must END the pin (`(?:[^\w.-]|$)`, consumed: RE2 has no lookahead), so
  // `@v1.2.3-rc1` is not read as 1.2.3 -- pin_re's `(?![\w.-])`.
  // depName is `mjdevaccount/<kit>` in every shape -- the same name the github-actions manager
  // gives a `uses:` -- so a kit release is ONE branch across workflows, scripts and pins (the
  // packageRule "first-party kits" below keeps the actions group from splitting it). Exception:
  // a uv git source (`ci-kit = { git = ..., tag = ... }`) is pep621's with depName
  // `ci-kit`; the per-kit groups at the end of packageRules put it in the SAME PR (2026-11-12,
  // a consumer's #2995 + #2996 were two heavy runs for one release), and pep621 still relocks it.
  // RELOCKED IN ACTIONS, NOT HERE: a regex manager rewrites pyproject.toml and leaves uv.lock
  // stale, and hosted Renovate runs no postUpgradeTasks. A uv repo whose kit pin only a regex
  // reads -- a release WHEEL (slim images with no git, so not a uv git source) or a PEP 508
  // `ci-kit @ git+` (pip / pre-commit installs ignore uv sources, and `ci-kit` is unclaimed on
  // PyPI) -- calls ci-kit's reusable `relock.yml`: on a renovate/ branch it runs `uv lock` and
  // pushes uv.lock with a push token so CI re-runs; `gitIgnoredAuthors` (top of this file) lets
  // Renovate still auto-merge that branch.
  // A dev-only uv GIT source in a repo nothing pip-installs needs no workflow: pep621 relocks it.
  // Until a repo is wired AND holds the token, its kit bump goes RED and waits -- its CI runs
  // `uv sync --locked` or `uv lock --check`. A repo that pins a kit in
  // pyproject without one of those must add it first, or a stale lock merges green.
  // A pin with NO lock beside it (a wheel URL in a workflow, a `run:` pip line)
  // auto-merges on green, so it can move ahead of the same kit in a uv repo still waiting on a
  // relock; nothing checks that two repos' pins agree.
  // uv.lock is never in managerFilePatterns: editing a URL there without its hash breaks the lock.
  customManagers: [
    {
      description: "first-party kit pinned by git URL tag (@vX.Y.Z) outside a `uses:` line",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/(^|/)package\\.json$/",
        "/(^|/)pyproject\\.toml$/",
        "/^\\.github/workflows/[^/]+\\.ya?ml$/",
        "/\\.(py|sh|bash|ps1|psm1)$/",
        "/(^|/)(Docker|Container)file[^/]*$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "https://github\\.com/(?<depName>mjdevaccount/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+?)(?:\\.git)?@v(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)(?:[^\\w.-]|$)",
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: "github-tags",
      versioningTemplate: "semver",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      description: "first-party kit pinned by GitHub release-asset URL (version in tag AND file name)",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/(^|/)pyproject\\.toml$/",
        "/^\\.github/workflows/[^/]+\\.ya?ml$/",
        "/\\.(py|sh|bash|ps1|psm1)$/",
        "/(^|/)(Docker|Container)file[^/]*$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "https://github\\.com/(?<depName>mjdevaccount/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)/releases/download/v(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)/[A-Za-z0-9_.]+-\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+-",
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: "github-releases",
      versioningTemplate: "semver",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      description: "first-party kit named in a .pin file as <kit>@vX.Y.Z (owner implied)",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: ["/\\.pin$/"],
      matchStrings: ["(?<kit>[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)@v(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)(?:[^\\w.-]|$)"],
      depNameTemplate: "mjdevaccount/{{{kit}}}",
      datasourceTemplate: "github-tags",
      versioningTemplate: "semver",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      // THIRD-PARTY release binaries a script pins by hand (2026-11-03, ci-kit v1.11.0: jscpd and
      // oasdiff in ci_kit/*.py). The standard Renovate annotation on the line ABOVE the version:
      //     # renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=oasdiff/oasdiff
      //     OASDIFF_VERSION = "1.35.0"
      // datasource and depName come from the comment, so one manager covers any tool and source.
      // A sha256 table beside the version is NOT moved by Renovate: the bump PR fails the tool's own
      // selftest on the digest (exit 2) until a session moves the hashes in the same PR. That is
      // deliberate -- no binary runs on a digest nobody read. ci-kit tests/
      // test_renovate_annotation_must_fire.py proves this pattern reads the shape and only it.
      description: "third-party version pinned under a `# renovate: datasource=... depName=...` comment",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/\\.(py|sh|bash|ps1|psm1|mjs)$/",
        "/^\\.github/workflows/[^/]+\\.ya?ml$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "#\\s*renovate:\\s*datasource=(?<datasource>[a-z-]+)\\s+depName=(?<depName>[^\\s]+)(?:\\s+versioning=(?<versioning>[^\\s]+))?[^\\n]*\\n[^\\n]*?[\"']?v?(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)(?:[^\\w.-]|$)",
      ],
      versioningTemplate: "{{#if versioning}}{{{versioning}}}{{else}}semver-coerced{{/if}}",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v?(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
  ],

  packageRules: [
    // ---- default: non-major lands on green, major waits for review ------------------------
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "pin", "digest", "pinDigest"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "major"],
    },

    // ---- a CI action's major lands on green, because CI RUNS IT ----------------------------
    // Measured 2026-10-17: 12 of 19 open Renovate PRs across two consumers were
    // action majors (checkout v7, cache v6, setup-uv v10, docker/* ...), every one CLEAN, none
    // read -- "major waits for review" with no reviewer is a queue, not a gate. An action's
    // major is exercised by the very PR that bumps it: if checkout v7 breaks, the PR goes RED
    // and the every-check rule above refuses it. That holds only for an action whose failure
    // is LOUD. The exceptions below are the ones whose failure is a GREEN.
    {
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    // ---- CI-ONLY TOOLING: no age wait, no weekend, majors included (2026-11-26) -------------
    // ci-kit#139 (Renovate validator 44 -> 45: the `# renovate: datasource=npm depName=renovate`
    // pin in ci.yml, a tool nothing but the `renovate config` job runs) sat behind
    // `renovate/stability-days`, then went CONFLICTED behind main, and was superseded by a hand
    // PR (#144). The operator: "why are we waiting on this and not just getting it done?" The 3-day
    // age wait is yank protection for something that SHIPS -- an image, a lockfile a service
    // imports. A CI tool ships nothing: it is exercised by the very PR that bumps it, a broken
    // one turns that PR red, and the every-check gate (header) refuses it. Waiting buys nothing
    // the PR's own run does not already prove, and the wait is what let the branch rot. So CI
    // tooling lands on green, any day (KIT_WINDOW: one PR a day per group, not one per release --
    // the first-party rules' Mend-run lesson below), majors included.
    // SCOPE, both rules: the `uses:` pins (depType action / reusable workflow -- NOT the docker
    // `container:`/`services:` images the same manager reads, which track a PRODUCTION major and
    // keep every rule below, the database-major hold first) and the `# renovate:` annotation pins
    // in a workflow or ci-kit's own gate binaries (ci_kit/*.py: oasdiff, jscpd, gitleaks -- each
    // with a must-fire selftest, and a sha256 table no bump moves, so its PR is red until a
    // session moves the hashes). NOT here: first-party kits (their own rules, below) and the
    // review gate's engine (`anthropics/claude-code-action` is held by name below; its CLI
    // `@anthropic-ai/claude-code` keeps the default: a gate graded by the edited copy is read
    // first). The fails-green exception BELOW still holds paths-filter / gitleaks-action majors
    // for a human (it comes AFTER these rules, so its `automerge: false` wins); these rules lift
    // only their wait. tests/test_renovate_ci_tooling.py pins scope, order and the db-major hold.
    {
      description: "CI-only tooling: an action / reusable-workflow `uses:` pin lands on green, no age wait",
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      matchDepTypes: ["action", "workflow"],
      matchPackageNames: ["!/^mjdevaccount\\//i", "!anthropics/claude-code-action"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
      schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    {
      description: "CI-only tooling: a `# renovate:` annotation pin in a workflow or ci-kit's gates lands on green, no age wait",
      matchManagers: ["custom.regex"],
      matchFileNames: [".github/workflows/**", "ci_kit/**"],
      matchPackageNames: ["!/^mjdevaccount\\//i", "!@anthropic-ai/claude-code"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
      schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"],
      automerge: true,
    },

    // ...except an action that DECIDES WHETHER OTHER CHECKS RUN (a broken paths-filter skips
    // the unit job, and a skipped step is a real green), or that IS a guard (a gitleaks that
    // scans nothing passes). Their failure looks exactly like success, so a human reads them.
    {
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      matchPackageNames: ["dorny/paths-filter", "gitleaks/gitleaks-action"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "major", "fails-green"],
    },


    // ---- grouping: one PR per ecosystem per week, not forty -------------------------------
    { matchManagers: ["github-actions"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "digest", "pinDigest"], groupName: "github actions" },
    // First-party kits: NOT in the actions group. A kit release is one branch per kit, keyed on
    // depName `mjdevaccount/<kit>`, holding its `uses:` lines AND the customManagers pins above --
    // grouped into "github actions" the `uses:` half would land apart from the rest, and
    // pin-drift reads every shape. (A pep621 uv git source of a kit, depName `ci-kit`, joins the
    // same PR through the per-kit groups at the end of this list.)
    // ANY DAY, NO AGE WAIT (2026-11-03: ci-kit v1.11.0 "Awaiting Schedule" on a Tuesday while
    // ci-pin-drift stayed red in every consumer). We publish these, so there is no yanked or
    // hijacked upstream to wait out, and a consumer's deploy fence covers the deploy risk the
    // weekend window exists for.
    // ONCE A DAY, NOT AT ANY TIME (v1.35.0, 2026-11-14): every day 00:00-11:59 ET (KIT_WINDOW; the
    // same value in every first-party rule below, tests/test_renovate_first_party_rules.py pins it). A
    // burst of kit releases on one day made one PR per release -- a full CI run in every consumer
    // and, in a queue repo, a place in the one-at-a-time lane. A daily window folds a day's releases
    // into ONE PR per kit per repo: in a queue repo the open PR is never re-pushed (`never`, the
    // last rule) so a newer release waits for the next window; elsewhere `updateNotScheduled`
    // (default true) moves the open PR to the newest release in place.
    // TWELVE HOURS, NOT FOUR (2026-11-20). The window is a filter on runs Mend starts, and Mend
    // runs a repo it does not consider hot only a few times a day at times of its choosing -- the
    // weekend window's lesson (top of file), relearned. 04:00-07:59 ET held trade-infra's ci-kit
    // v1.42.0 bump "Awaiting Schedule" for four days: its Mend runs (Dependency Dashboard edits,
    // ET) fell at 02:29/08:05 (11-16), 02:24/13:42 (11-17), 00:51/08:08/16:08 (11-18) and
    // 00:27/09:06 (11-19) -- two of them 5 and 8 minutes past the window -- and the PR opened only
    // on 11-20 at 05:48. ci-pin-drift (48h grace) went red 11-18, 11-19 and 11-20. Every one of
    // those days had a run inside 00:00-11:59. Still before the afternoon: a consumer's deploy
    // fence covers the RTH half of the morning, as it covers the weekend window's deploys.
    // ci-pin-drift's grace is 48h for this (ci_kit/pin_drift.py): two windows to land in.
    {
      description: "first-party kits",
      matchPackageNames: ["/^mjdevaccount\\//i"],
      groupName: null,
      schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },
    // By DATASOURCE, not manager (2026-11-14): which manager parses a pyproject is Renovate's call,
    // not ours. A plain `[project]` file with no lock is read by `poetry`, not `pep621` (renovate
    // 44 dry-run on a consumer: "Found poetry package files", one branch per package), so the
    // manager-named group never formed and that consumer got 12 PRs at once. Every Python
    // manager (pep621, poetry, pip_requirements, pip-compile, pep723, pipenv) looks up `pypi`.
    // First-party packages leave it through the LATER rules below; majors were never in it.
    // groupSlug: the name's slug keeps the parentheses (dry-run branch `renovate/python-(non-major)`),
    // a branch name an unquoted shell line breaks on.
    { matchDatasources: ["pypi"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"], groupName: "python (non-major)", groupSlug: "python-non-major" },
    { matchManagers: ["npm"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"], groupName: "npm (non-major)" },
    { matchManagers: ["nuget"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"], groupName: "nuget (non-major)" },
    { matchManagers: ["dockerfile"], groupName: "dockerfile base images" },
    // First-party packages PUBLISHED TO A REGISTRY, matched by source repo, not name (2026-11-04).
    // A consumer moved a first-party library from a release-wheel URL (depName `<owner>/<kit>`,
    // caught by "first-party kits" above) to PyPI (depName `<kit>`), and the rule stopped
    // matching: v1.1.0 sat "Pending Status Checks" behind the 3-day age wait inside the weekly
    // "python (non-major)" group while ci-pin-drift was red on the day it shipped. The pypi/npm
    // datasources read the package's repository URL, so this follows a kit to ANY registry under
    // ANY name. AFTER the grouping rules on purpose: a later packageRule wins, and the python
    // group would otherwise fold a kit bump into the weekly PR.
    {
      description: "first-party packages on a registry (e.g. PyPI)",
      matchSourceUrls: ["https://github.com/mjdevaccount/**"],
      groupName: null,
      schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },
    // ONE PR PER KIT PER RELEASE (2026-11-12). With groupName null, Renovate keys a branch on
    // depName, and a kit has TWO: `mjdevaccount/ci-kit` (a `uses:` line, the customManagers pins)
    // and `ci-kit` (a consumer's uv git source, pep621). ci-kit v1.25.0 opened a consumer's #2995
    // "update dependency ci-kit" AND #2996 "update mjdevaccount/ci-kit action", each a full heavy
    // unit run on that consumer's ONE heavy runner. A named group spans managers, so both depNames
    // land in one branch. ONE matcher, the source URL (two matchers in one rule must BOTH match):
    // github-tags (the `uses:` line and the pins) and git-tags (the uv git source) both report
    // https://github.com/mjdevaccount/<kit>, which is how the rule above already caught #2995.
    // Not bare depNames: npm has unrelated packages under the same bare names. Same holds as the two rules above
    // (any time, no age wait). AFTER them on purpose: a later rule wins.
    { description: "first-party kit ci-kit, one PR a day", matchSourceUrls: ["https://github.com/mjdevaccount/ci-kit", "https://github.com/mjdevaccount/ci-kit.git"], groupName: "ci-kit", schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"], minimumReleaseAge: null },
    { description: "first-party kit edge-platform, one PR a day", matchSourceUrls: ["https://github.com/mjdevaccount/edge-platform", "https://github.com/mjdevaccount/edge-platform.git"], groupName: "edge-platform", schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"], minimumReleaseAge: null },
    { description: "first-party kit llm-ports, one PR a day", matchSourceUrls: ["https://github.com/mjdevaccount/llm-ports", "https://github.com/mjdevaccount/llm-ports.git"], groupName: "llm-ports", schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"], minimumReleaseAge: null },
    { description: "first-party kit market-ports, one PR a day", matchSourceUrls: ["https://github.com/mjdevaccount/market-ports", "https://github.com/mjdevaccount/market-ports.git"], groupName: "market-ports", schedule: ["* 0-11 * * *"], minimumReleaseAge: null },

    // ---- container images in compose files DEPLOY ON MERGE ---------------------------------
    // A consumer's compose may be shipped by a deploy reconciler within the hour, unattended.
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      groupName: "compose images",
      labels: ["deps", "deploys-on-merge"],
    },
    // A compose MINOR lands on green too (2026-10-23). Until then a minor
    // waited for review, and with one operator "waits for review" meant the Dependency Dashboard
    // grew and nothing landed. Most compose minors are observability (Prometheus, Loki, Tempo,
    // Grafana): a bad bump costs telemetry, not data. The stateful exceptions are held
    // by LATER rules -- "database images" below, and the langfuse / windmill rules at the end --
    // so this rule sits HERE, before them, rather than at the end where it would win over them.
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    // A compose MAJOR is never grouped: each one is its own architecture decision, and a group
    // makes whoever merges one ship them all. Measured 2026-10-18: a consumer PR bundled
    // Langfuse 2 -> 3 (ClickHouse/Redis/S3, owned by the AI-suite thread) with Tempo 2 -> 3,
    // which that consumer had recorded as deliberately NOT taken. The Langfuse merge
    // would have deployed Tempo 3 unattended within the hour. One PR per major, each read
    // on its own. (A database image's major stays in "database images" below: later rule wins.)
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      groupName: null,
    },
    // A FLOATING tag pinned by digest (litellm:main-stable@sha256:...) has no version in the
    // tag, so every move is a "digest" update to Renovate -- and digest updates auto-merge
    // above. Measured 2026-10-10: the box ran litellm 1.91.1 behind that tag while upstream
    // was 1.104.2, so the first Renovate pass would have auto-landed thirteen minor releases
    // of the LLM gateway (config.yaml schema included) as one unattended digest bump. A
    // digest move on a floating tag is a release, not a rebuild; it waits for review.
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/berriai/litellm"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["digest"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "deploys-on-merge", "floating-tag"],
    },

    // ---- the review gate's own action never auto-merges -----------------------------------
    // A consumer's `review gate` (review-gate.yml) is a required check that is GRADED BY THE
    // EDITED WORKFLOW: a PR that changes review-gate.yml is judged by the version it carries.
    // That hole is known for human PRs; Renovate would make it recurring, because the
    // action's SHA bump rides the auto-merging "github actions" group and lands in that file.
    // So the pin on the gate's action is the one actions update a human reads first.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["anthropics/claude-code-action"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "review-gate"],
    },

    // Dockerfile bumps are NOT held, and that is safe ONLY in a repo whose CI builds its images
    // on the PR -- with platformAutomerge off above, that check gates the merge whether or not
    // branch protection requires it. THIS PRESET IS SHARED, so the claim is per repo. Until
    // 2026-10-17 one consumer built nothing on the PR, and its #608 (python:3.14) auto-merged
    // green, failed the first build on the host and wedged its deploy reconciler for 13h.
    // A consumer whose images are built only post-merge is exposed unless its bases float with
    // no digest (then the sole update is a major, which it can turn off in its own renovate.json);
    // pinning a digest there arms the auto-merge.
    // A repo with a Dockerfile that extends this preset adds its PR build FIRST, or Renovate
    // auto-merges an image nobody built.

    // ---- the database is never automatic ---------------------------------------------------
    // A Postgres / Timescale image change is a dba window (pg_upgrade, extension versions,
    // pgbackrest compatibility), never a Saturday auto-merge.
    {
      matchPackageNames: [
        "timescale/timescaledb-ha",
        "postgres",
        "edoburu/pgbouncer",
        "timescale/pgai-vectorizer-worker",
        "woblerr/pgbackrest_exporter",
      ],
      groupName: "database images",
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "dba"],
    },
    {
      // pg18.4-ts2.28.3-all: Postgres major/minor, then the Timescale version.
      matchPackageNames: ["timescale/timescaledb-ha"],
      versioning: "regex:^pg(?<major>\\d+)\\.(?<minor>\\d+)-ts(?<patch>\\d+)\\.(?<build>\\d+)\\.(?<revision>\\d+)-(?<compatibility>.+)$",
    },
    // ---- a database-image MAJOR is HELD FOR APPROVAL, by name (2026-11-26) --------------------
    // A Postgres major is a pg_upgrade of a stateful cluster, not a tag bump. Until now nothing
    // said that: a consumer's "update database images to v19" (langfuse-pg 18.6 -> 19.0, the
    // backup sidecar's pg_dump client 18 -> 19) and edge-platform's CI service container
    // (postgres 18 -> 19) sat in "Pending Status Checks" from 2026-11-20 -- the dashboard section
    // that reads "a wait that will end". It would not: the docker datasource takes the release
    // timestamp from Docker Hub's `tag_last_pushed` (renovate lib/modules/datasource/docker/
    // index.ts, releaseTimestampNote), and docker-library re-pushes its floating tags every few
    // days (11-26: `19` pushed 11-26, `19-alpine` 11-23, `18-alpine` 11-21), so the 3-day age
    // wait re-armed under every rebuild -- the python base-image rule's class. Had it ever ended,
    // the PR it opened would have been held only by `automerge: false`, with nothing naming why.
    // Now the hold is a NAMED STATE: dependencyDashboardApproval puts the update under the
    // dashboard's "Pending Approval" with no branch and no PR (docs/usage/key-concepts/
    // dashboard.md, "Require approval for major updates"), and the suffix names the gate on the
    // dashboard line itself. Ticking the box is the dba's act, AFTER the gate below is met; it
    // opens a PR that still never merges itself. The age wait is lifted for the same reason as
    // the python rule: it is yank protection for an UNATTENDED merge, and an approval behind a
    // written plan, weeks after GA, is the opposite of one -- with it, the approved PR would sit
    // pending again for the next re-push. The gate, checked 2026-11-26: TimescaleDB 2.32.1 (the
    // newest release) builds against PG 15-18 only (CMakeLists.txt at the tag; PG19 is on main,
    // 8078c3520 "Add PG 19.0 to ci", unreleased) and timescaledb-ha has no pg19 tag (Docker Hub
    // tags?name=pg19: count 0); pgvector tests 19 on main; the pgai repo is ARCHIVED (last
    // extension release 0.11.2, 2025-10). mcp scripts/renovate_liveness.py watches the hold from
    // outside: every "Pending Approval" line's age, and the gate turning true (that tag appearing).
    // Later rules (pgbouncer patch, postgres 17 cap) never match a major, so these stand.
    //
    // ONE APPROVAL = ONE DECISION. Approval is per BRANCH, and the "database images" group would
    // put every db image's major on one: ticking a pgbouncer major would have meant approving a
    // pg_upgrade. So the hold ungroups (groupName null: one branch per image), and only the
    // SERVER images -- postgres and timescaledb-ha, the two that ARE a cluster major and that the
    // consumer's pg_dump client must track -- are re-grouped, with the pg_upgrade gate. prBodyNotes
    // is MERGEABLE in Renovate (lib/config/options/index.ts), so a server major carries both notes.
    {
      description: "database-image MAJOR: held for Dependency Dashboard approval, one branch per image",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "timescale/timescaledb-ha",
        "postgres",
        "edoburu/pgbouncer",
        "timescale/pgai-vectorizer-worker",
        "woblerr/pgbackrest_exporter",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: true,
      automerge: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
      groupName: null,
      commitMessageSuffix: "(held for dba approval)",
      labels: ["deps", "dba", "major"],
      prBodyNotes: [
        "**Database-image MAJOR -- approved from the Dependency Dashboard, never merged by Renovate** (ci-kit renovate/default.json5, database-image major rule). Read the release notes for config and protocol changes, and move it only once the server it fronts supports it.",
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "database SERVER major (postgres, timescaledb-ha): one pg_upgrade decision, gate = every extension released for the new major + a written upgrade plan",
      matchPackageNames: ["timescale/timescaledb-ha", "postgres"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      groupName: "database server images",
      groupSlug: "database-server-images",
      commitMessageSuffix: "(held for dba: extension support + upgrade plan)",
      prBodyNotes: [
        "**A pg_upgrade, never a merge-and-deploy.** Before this PR moves: (1) every extension the cluster loads (timescaledb, pgvector, ai/pgai, ...) has a RELEASED build for the new major, and a `timescale/timescaledb-ha` tag exists for it; (2) a written upgrade plan exists -- the pg_upgrade drill, rollback and ANALYZE steps (in trade-infra the PG17 -> 18 runbook is docs/DB-DISK-MIGRATION.md); (3) the coupled images move WITH the server, never ahead of it: the consumer's backup pg_dump client image and its CI/test service containers track the PRODUCTION major (in trade-infra: infra/backup/Dockerfile).",
      ],
    },
    // ---- a TimescaleDB EXTENSION major is the same hold (2026-11-26) ---------------------------
    // The versioning regex above maps only the POSTGRES major to `major`: ts2 -> ts3 lands in the
    // `patch` slot, so matchUpdateTypes ["major"] never sees it, and it would get the plain
    // "database images" group -- yet it is an ALTER EXTENSION timescaledb on the live cluster and
    // a data-format change. Matched by VALUE instead (matchCurrentValue / matchNewValue, both
    // regex-or-glob with `!` negation, lib/util/package-rules/{current,new}-value.ts): the
    // current tag on ts2 and the new one not. It names ts2 because no regex can compare the two
    // fields; the PR note says to move it to the next major in the change that adopts it, and a
    // test pins it to the series the estate runs (pg18.7-ts2.32.1-all, 11-26). Side effect, by
    // the versioning: once a ts3 tag exists it is the highest `patch`, so ts2.x bumps stop being
    // offered until it is approved -- the dashboard line says why.
    {
      description: "TimescaleDB EXTENSION major (ts2 -> ts3+): held for Dependency Dashboard approval",
      matchPackageNames: ["timescale/timescaledb-ha"],
      matchCurrentValue: "/-ts2\\./",
      matchNewValue: "!/-ts2\\./",
      dependencyDashboardApproval: true,
      automerge: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
      groupName: "timescaledb extension major",
      groupSlug: "timescaledb-extension-major",
      commitMessageSuffix: "(held for dba: ALTER EXTENSION plan)",
      labels: ["deps", "dba", "major"],
      prBodyNotes: [
        "**TimescaleDB EXTENSION major -- an ALTER EXTENSION timescaledb on the live cluster, never a merge-and-deploy** (ci-kit renovate/default.json5, TimescaleDB extension major rule). Before this PR moves: the extension's upgrade notes are read, a written plan exists (ALTER EXTENSION, rollback = restore, continuous aggregates and compression checked), and the same change moves that ci-kit rule from ts2 to the new major.",
      ],
    },

    // ---- single-operator self-drive (2026-10-23) ---------------------------------------------
    // These come LAST on purpose: in Renovate a later matching rule wins, so each one below
    // overrides the broad groups and auto-merge defaults above.

    // Langfuse is STATEFUL: it runs Prisma and ClickHouse migrations at startup, so a bump is
    // a one-way schema change. One PR for the four images it moves with, read before merging.
    // `held-deploy`, NOT `deploys-on-merge` (2026-11-14): the consumer's deploy policy recreates
    // none of these on its own (manual), so a merge recreates nothing; the bump needs a deliberate
    // recreate under a held deploy. The label must say what the merge DOES, not what a deploy might.
    // GREEN MERGES (2026-11-28, operator: "isnt that the point of coded tests to check this shit?
    // they are green"). The HOLD lives in the DEPLOY, not the merge: the consumer's deploy
    // reconciler ships a merged held-deploy pin unattended through its drain-safe held deploy
    // (edge-host runbooks/lib-held-infra.sh: hold -> drain -> deploy -> verify -> release, one
    // stateful unit per slot, a failed drain defers, never forced) and pages a pin merged but not
    // running for 12h (home-host HeldDeployOwedStuck). So `automerge` is true and `held-deploy`
    // stays as an INFORMATIONAL label: nothing reads it. The lane picks what to deploy from its own
    // unit list (edge-host scripts/infra_config.py HELD_UNITS) vs the running containers, so a NEW
    // stateful image added here must ALSO be added there, or it merges on green and nothing ships it. MAJORS stay held: the default major rule
    // (automerge false) is not overridden here -- this rule matches minor/patch/digest only.
    {
      matchPackageNames: [
        "langfuse/langfuse",
        "langfuse/langfuse-worker",
        "clickhouse/clickhouse-server",
        "redis",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "digest"],
      groupName: "langfuse stack",
      automerge: true,
      labels: ["deps", "held-deploy", "stateful"],
    },
    // ---- images NOT on Docker Hub (ghcr.io first): no release timestamp, so no END to the age wait --
    // Renovate reads no release timestamp from ghcr.io, and minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour's default
    // (timestamp-required) treats that as "not old enough" FOREVER: the update sits under the
    // Dependency Dashboard's "Pending Status Checks" with no branch, no PR and nothing red.
    // Seen three times, fixed twice one package at a time: windmill (this rule's old home),
    // a git-ref pin (first-party rule below), and a consumer's `dockerfile base images`
    // group (ghcr.io/actions/actions-runner + semaphore) pending since 2026-11-03 -- surfaced
    // only by a Renovate liveness check outside the kit. One rule for the REGISTRY,
    // not the package, so the next ghcr image does not wait a week to be noticed.
    // What is given up: the 3-day age guard for ghcr images. CI still gates every merge, the
    // weekend window still applies, and a wait that cannot end guarded nothing.
    // EVERY REGISTRY THAT IS NOT DOCKER HUB, not ghcr alone (2026-11-26). Renovate's docker
    // datasource reads a release timestamp from Docker Hub ONLY (minimum-release-age.md,
    // "Which registries support release timestamps?": "(not Docker Hub)" is unsupported, renovate#38656; ghcr
    // #39064, quay #38572, all open), so quay.io, lscr.io, registry.k8s.io and mcr.microsoft.com
    // were in the same hold the ghcr rule lifted -- and a WORSE one: inside a group with any
    // releasable member, Renovate DROPS the pending upgrade (updates/generate.ts, "Branch is not
    // pending, removing pending upgrades"), so it is not even listed under Pending Status Checks,
    // only as `[Updates: ...]` under Detected Dependencies. Measured 11-26: a consumer's
    // quay.io blackbox-exporter v0.29.0 -> v0.29.1 (released 10-21) and registry.k8s.io pause
    // 3.10.2 in no branch; another consumer bumped the same blackbox by hand (10-31) and its
    // quay.io postgres-exporter v0.21.1 (11-18) is in no branch. The host test is Docker's own
    // (a first path segment with "." or ":", or localhost); Docker Hub spelled with its host
    // (docker.io/, index.docker.io/, registry-1.docker.io/) keeps the age gate.
    // TIMESTAMP-OPTIONAL, NOT minimumReleaseAge: null: identical today (no timestamp = no wait),
    // but a registry that starts publishing timestamps re-arms the 3-day wait by itself, with no
    // edit here. Its one cost is the dashboard WARN, which `logLevelRemap` (top) demotes.
    {
      matchDatasources: ["docker"],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "/^(localhost|[^/]*[.:][^/]*)\\//",
        "!docker.io/**",
        "!index.docker.io/**",
        "!registry-1.docker.io/**",
      ],
      minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour: "timestamp-optional",
    },
    // LINUXSERVER IMAGES NEVER MOVED (2026-11-26). Their tags are `v1.15.1-ls178`, and docker
    // versioning reads everything after the first hyphen as a COMPATIBILITY suffix and proposes
    // only tags with the SAME suffix (versioning/docker/readme.md). Every linuxserver build bumps
    // `-lsNNN`, so no candidate ever matched: a consumer's lscr.io speedtest-tracker sat on
    // v1.15.1-ls178 with no `[Updates: ...]` at all, which the registry rule above cannot fix.
    // Version = semver + the ls build as `build` (Renovate's own regex-versioning readme example
    // for ghcr.io/linuxserver/tautulli). A tag of another shape (`latest`, a date) is NOT valid
    // under this regex and is skipped -- none is pinned today; such an image needs its own rule.
    {
      matchDatasources: ["docker"],
      matchPackageNames: ["lscr.io/linuxserver/**", "ghcr.io/linuxserver/**", "linuxserver/**", "docker.io/linuxserver/**"],
      versioning: "regex:^v?(?<major>\\d+)\\.(?<minor>\\d+)\\.(?<patch>\\d+)-ls(?<build>\\d+)$",
    },
    // Windmill server (compose) and workers (worker.Dockerfile FROM) MUST share one version, so
    // one group spans both managers. Its ghcr age wait is the rule above. A minor/patch MERGES on
    // green and deploys under the consumer's held deploy (langfuse stack rule above: the hold is
    // in the deploy); a MAJOR does not -- Windmill documents some major migrations as manual, and
    // the consumer's unattended lane refuses a windmill major too (a human upgrade).
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/windmill-labs/windmill"],
      groupName: "windmill",
      automerge: true,
      labels: ["deps", "held-deploy"],
    },
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/windmill-labs/windmill"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      automerge: false,
    },
    // The rest of one consumer's compose images that its deploy policy does NOT let
    // a merge recreate (2026-11-14, checked service by service against that policy): caddy is
    // `manual, recreate_ok=False` (the blue/green colour lives in the reload process), crawl4ai
    // (mid-research calls) and valkey (litellm's cache) are unpolicied = manual. In "compose images"
    // they were labelled `deploys-on-merge` and merging recreated nothing. Their OWN group, because
    // Renovate unions every upgrade's labels into its PR (workers/repository/updates/generate.ts),
    // so inside "compose images" the PR would carry both labels. Merge policy is unchanged (the
    // compose rules above decide automerge); the deploy policy's `manual` finding is the WARN that
    // names the recreate still owed. Majors keep their own PRs (groupName null above), relabelled.
    // database images (pg-batch, langfuse-pg/-db, pgai), windmill, langfuse and minio have rules.
    // crawl4ai and valkey LEFT this rule (2026-11-28): the consumer's policy recreates both now,
    // drained (crawl4ai 2026-11-15, valkey 2026-11-23, edge-host scripts/infra_config.py POLICY),
    // so a merge DOES recreate them and "compose images" (deploys-on-merge) is their true label.
    // caddy stays: its non-major bumps merge on green (the compose defaults) and the consumer's
    // held deploy recreates it (langfuse stack rule above).
    {
      matchRepositories: ["mjdevaccount/trade-infra"],
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchPackageNames: ["caddy"],
      labels: ["deps", "held-deploy"],
    },
    {
      matchRepositories: ["mjdevaccount/trade-infra"],
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchPackageNames: ["caddy"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "digest", "pin", "pinDigest"],
      groupName: "compose images (held-deploy)",
      groupSlug: "compose-images-held-deploy",
    },
    // A Python base-image bump changes the interpreter under every image built on it (#608:
    // python:3.14 auto-merged and wedged a deploy reconciler for 13h). Its own PR, read first.
    // NO AGE WAIT HERE EITHER (2026-10-31): the official `python:X.Y-slim` tag is
    // REBUILT every time Debian slim moves (3.15-slim: created 2026-10-28, ~weekly), and Renovate's
    // docker datasource reads the rebuild's `created` as the release timestamp -- so a 3-day
    // minimumReleaseAge RE-ARMS on every rebuild and the update sits in "Pending Status Checks"
    // with no branch and no PR (a consumer's dashboard, Friday 2026-10-30: python 3.15 pending,
    // 02:00Z 10-31 it cleared, and the next rebuild would have re-pended it). The age guard is
    // yank protection for an UNATTENDED merge. A python MINOR or major never auto-merges here, so
    // for it the wait only hides the PR a session must read. A DIGEST or PATCH DOES auto-merge (the
    // next rule, 2026-11-28) and so DELIBERATELY gives up the yank window: with the age gate on, a
    // rebuild every ~week re-arms it and a digest would never merge at all. Its guard instead is the
    // image built ON THE PR (the table above): a base that breaks the build is a red check. The
    // weekend window still applies.
    {
      matchManagers: ["dockerfile"],
      matchPackageNames: ["python"],
      groupName: "python base image",
      automerge: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },
    // A python DIGEST or PATCH merges on green (2026-11-28, operator: green tests = merge). A digest
    // is the same X.Y interpreter rebuilt on a newer Debian slim; a patch is the interpreter's own
    // bugfix release. Neither is the #608 class (a new X.Y under every image), and every repo on this
    // rule builds its images ON THE PR (the Dockerfile note above), so a base that breaks the build
    // is a red check the every-check gate refuses. Measured: a consumer's #968 (python:3.15-slim
    // digest, its `images` check green) sat CLEAN from 05:20Z to 13:08Z, read by nobody, until a
    // session merged it by hand. A MINOR (3.15 -> 3.16) or major stays held: still Disabled above.
    // A group holding a minor AND a digest is expected to stay held (UNVERIFIED against Renovate's
    // docs: merged-config automerge is per upgrade; the PR body's `Automerge:` line is what the queue
    // reads, ci_kit/pr_renovate.py, so whatever Renovate decides there is what lands or waits).
    {
      matchManagers: ["dockerfile"],
      matchPackageNames: ["python"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["digest", "patch", "pinDigest"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    // ---- cgr.dev/chainguard/minio:latest: a digest the age gate can NEVER release (2026-11-12) --
    // Chainguard's free minio has ONLY `latest`/`latest-dev` (cgr.dev tags/list, 11-12), so the pin
    // is `latest@sha256:...` and every move is a digest update. Renovate ages a digest update
    // against the matched VERSION's timestamp, reads timestamps from Docker Hub only, and holds an
    // unversioned tag on any other registry "indefinitely" under timestamp-required
    // (docs.renovatebot.com/key-concepts/minimum-release-age/, "digest updates", read 11-12 for
    // 44.236.5). Measured: pinned since 10-18, zero Renovate PRs in 25 days, a consumer's
    // dashboard lists both langfuse-minio entries `[Updates: latest]` in no branch.
    // Cadence would block it anyway: 34 index builds 10-01..11-12, median gap 0.74 days.
    // The age gate is yank protection for an UNATTENDED merge, so it is REPLACED, not just lifted:
    // its own PR (in "compose images" it would carry the held-deploy label into that group),
    // opened in the weekend window and NOT re-pushed between windows, so the digest CI judged is
    // the one that lands. Merges on green since 2026-11-28 (langfuse stack rule above: the hold is
    // in the deploy): the consumer's held deploy recreates langfuse-minio and -minio-ilm under a
    // drain, and only for the digest of an image it names (edge-host scripts/held_infra.py).
    // Until then: no auto-merge (minio holds langfuse's event store, a data volume; a `latest`
    // move can be a minio release, same reasoning as the litellm floating-tag rule).
    {
      matchDatasources: ["docker"],
      matchPackageNames: ["cgr.dev/chainguard/minio"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["digest"],
      groupName: "chainguard minio",
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
      automerge: true,
      updateNotScheduled: false,
      labels: ["deps", "held-deploy", "floating-tag", "stateful"],
    },
    // A pgbouncer PATCH lands on green: CI runs scripts/test_pgbouncer_lan.sh against the
    // image, so a broken patch is a red check. After "database images" so it wins for patches.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["edoburu/pgbouncer"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["patch"],
      groupName: "pgbouncer",
      automerge: true,
    },
    // langfuse-db is the Langfuse v2 ROLLBACK database (postgres:17, nothing writes it now). It
    // never moves to 18 -- a consumer PR was closed for exactly that.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["postgres"],
      matchCurrentValue: "/^17/",
      allowedVersions: "<18",
    },
    // Redis stays on 7 until the Langfuse thread picks Redis 8 or the valkey already in the
    // stack. Redis 8 also changed its license.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["redis"],
      allowedVersions: "<8",
    },
    // ClickHouse follows the series LANGFUSE recommends, not ClickHouse's newest (2026-10-30). It
    // exists here only as Langfuse v4's store, and Langfuse documents "25.12 minimum, 26.4
    // recommended" (langfuse-docs self-hosting/upgrade/upgrade-guides/upgrade-v3-to-v4.mdx)
    // while its own docker-compose.yml still pins 25.12 at v4.72.0. A consumer PR grouped
    // 26.4.5 -> 26.10.3: past the 26.8 LTS and 26.9's mandatory analyzer, after Langfuse had
    // already broken on 26.6's analyzer (langfuse#15125, #15977) and on 26.8's DateTime64 parsing
    // (#16858). ClickHouse does not promise a downgrade, so a bad jump is a restore, not a revert.
    // Raise this cap when Langfuse's recommended series moves -- in the same PR that cites it.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["clickhouse/clickhouse-server"],
      allowedVersions: "<26.5",
    },

    // ---- first-party packages skip the age wait (2026-10-24) -------------------------------
    // minimumReleaseAge guards against a THIRD party's broken or hijacked publish being yanked
    // within hours. Our own tags (ci-kit, a first-party preset, a sibling repo pinned by commit)
    // are cut by the same sessions that consume them, and a bad one is fixed by the next tag, not
    // a yank. The wait also never ENDS for some of them: a git-refs pin (a `.ref` file naming a
    // sibling repo's commit) carries no release timestamp, and minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour's
    // default (timestamp-required) treats that as "not old enough" forever -- it sat in
    // "Pending Status Checks" from 10-18 with no branch and no PR. The weekend window still
    // applies; only the 3-day wait is lifted. Both package-name shapes: "owner/repo" (actions,
    // github-tags) and the URL form (git-refs, uv git sources).
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["/^(https://github\\.com/)?mjdevaccount//i"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },

    // ---- a repo with a MERGE QUEUE: Renovate never merges (2026-11-12) ----------------------
    // EVERY SERIAL-QUEUE REPO, and Renovate never REBASES on behind either (2026-11-14). The queue
    // is ci-kit's pr-autoupdate with max-in-flight 1 (+ merge-train in some): one armed PR
    // made current with main at a time, lowest number first. Renovate was a SECOND updater beside
    // it: its own rebase (rebaseWhen auto = behind-base-branch under strict) made a consumer's #3072
    // current with main while #3073, the queue's one in-flight PR, was mid-review; #3072 landed
    // at 03:53:47Z and #3073/#3075/#3076 all went BEHIND. So in these repos a Renovate branch is
    // rebased only on a CONFLICT, merged by nothing of its own, and becomes current with main
    // only when the queue picks it (ci-kit pr-autoupdate ARMS it on its next pass,
    // ci_kit/pr_renovate.py since v1.32.0 -- no hand step; an outside PR reconciler is the witness).
    // A PR ALREADY CLEAN (every check green, current with main) cannot be armed -- GitHub refuses
    // "clean status" -- so with the queue IDLE (nothing in flight or next, no train) pr-autoupdate
    // MERGES the lowest one itself, pinned to its head (v1.61.0, pr_renovate.land_idle): trade-infra
    // #977/#978 sat CLEAN under "armed once main moves" on a quiet Saturday until a hand merge,
    // 2026-11-28. UNSTABLE (stability-days still pending, or any red check) is never landed. A repo that adds pr-autoupdate.yml with
    // max-in-flight joins this list (tests/test_renovate_merge_queue.py pins it).
    // A consumer runs ci-kit's merge train + serial pr-autoupdate, which land armed PRs lowest
    // number first, one heavy CI run at a time. Renovate's own merge sees none of it: its
    // #2995 was merged BY RENOVATE at 03:21:47Z, two minutes after a session ran
    // `gh pr merge --disable-auto` and added `autoland-off`; it jumped the queue, moved main under
    // #2981 and cost a full heavy re-run. `platformAutomerge: true` does NOT stop that (Renovate
    // 44.221.1 source, workers/repository/update/pr/automerge.js): with `automerge` on, every pass
    // whose branch is green still calls platform.mergePr itself, and reattemptPlatformAutomerge
    // re-arms a PR a human disarmed. So here Renovate's merge step is `pr-comment`: when green it
    // only posts the comment below. `automerge` stays true, so the PR body still reads
    // "**Automerge**: Enabled" -- the verdict ci_kit/pr_renovate.py reads to ARM it (squash, the
    // GraphQL arm); the queue then lands it like any armed PR, and `--disable-auto` / `autoland-off`
    // hold it. `automerge: false` (database images, majors, `fails-green`) reads Disabled: never armed. The every-check gate the header describes becomes the REQUIRED checks here, the
    // standard every other PR in the repo already merges under.
    {
      description: "serial-queue repos: Renovate never merges or rebases on behind; the consumer arms, the queue lands",
      matchRepositories: [
        "mjdevaccount/trade-data", "mjdevaccount/trade-infra", "mjdevaccount/trade-ui",
        "mjdevaccount/edge-host", "mjdevaccount/home-host", "mjdevaccount/TradingEdge.Scraper",
        "mjdevaccount/claude-plugins", "mjdevaccount/local-mcp", "mjdevaccount/mattpc-llm-stack",
        "mjdevaccount/cred-broker", "mjdevaccount/ci-kit",
      ],
      rebaseWhen: "conflicted",
      automergeType: "pr-comment",
      automergeComment: "Renovate does not merge PRs in this repository (ci-kit renovate/default.json5, merge-queue repos): ci-kit pr-autoupdate arms this PR and the merge train / pr-autoupdate queue lands it in PR-number order. `autoland-off` holds it.",
    },
    // ---- the weekly lock PR in a queue repo waits as-is outside its window (v1.35.0) -----------
    // Scoped HERE, not on lockFileMaintenance: see the window comment there. In these repos
    // Renovate never merges (`pr-comment` above), so skipping the branch pass outside the window
    // costs nothing but the re-push: the open lock PR is not regenerated mid merge-train compose.
    {
      description: "serial-queue repos: the weekly lock PR is not re-pushed outside its window",
      matchRepositories: [
        "mjdevaccount/trade-data", "mjdevaccount/trade-infra", "mjdevaccount/trade-ui",
        "mjdevaccount/edge-host", "mjdevaccount/home-host", "mjdevaccount/TradingEdge.Scraper",
        "mjdevaccount/claude-plugins", "mjdevaccount/local-mcp", "mjdevaccount/mattpc-llm-stack",
        "mjdevaccount/cred-broker", "mjdevaccount/ci-kit",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["lockFileMaintenance"],
      updateNotScheduled: false,
    },
    // ---- a first-party kit branch in a queue repo is pushed ONCE (2026-11-14) -----------------
    // `conflicted` above did not stop the #1 runner waste: a consumer's renovate/ci-kit ran 194 CI
    // runs / 469 job-min on 11-13 and 96 / 223 on 11-14, and only ~1/6 of it was a release's first
    // push. The rest was a PING-PONG: Renovate's commit carries pyproject + workflows but no
    // uv.lock, a relock workflow adds the lock as github-actions[bot] (in gitIgnoredAuthors, so the
    // branch still reads as Renovate's own), and on its next run Renovate finds the branch differs
    // from what it would write and recreates it on current main -- `conflicted` does not cover that
    // path (lib/workers/repository/update/branch/reuse.ts: only `never` returns `skip-update`
    // before the fingerprint/content check). Each recreate re-ran CI, then the relock re-ran it.
    // 11-14: re-pushes of an already-pushed version 33 runs / 77 min, relocks 35 / 96; 11-13: 77 /
    // 149 and 84 / 262. `never` = one push + one relock per PR; the queue (pr-autoupdate) is what
    // makes it current with main, as for every PR here. A newer release while the PR is open waits
    // for it to land (the next run opens the next PR; pin-drift's 48h grace covers the gap). A
    // CONFLICT is not stranded: Renovate never moves this branch by itself, so ci-kit pr-autoupdate
    // ticks the PR's rebase box (ci_kit/pr_renovate.py) and Renovate recreates it once. Scoped to
    // the queue repos: elsewhere nothing else updates a branch, and Renovate's own rebase is the updater.
    {
      description: "serial-queue repos: a first-party kit branch is never re-pushed by Renovate (no relock ping-pong)",
      matchRepositories: [
        "mjdevaccount/trade-data", "mjdevaccount/trade-infra", "mjdevaccount/trade-ui",
        "mjdevaccount/edge-host", "mjdevaccount/home-host", "mjdevaccount/TradingEdge.Scraper",
        "mjdevaccount/claude-plugins", "mjdevaccount/local-mcp", "mjdevaccount/mattpc-llm-stack",
        "mjdevaccount/cred-broker", "mjdevaccount/ci-kit",
      ],
      matchSourceUrls: ["https://github.com/mjdevaccount/**"],
      rebaseWhen: "never",
    },
  ],
}
